  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss over x, z from −2 to 2. It is 4 times steeper in x, so overshoot shows there first. Without noise, x diverges when the learning rate is above 0.5.",
    "Each update: step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with mean zero and sd 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, so it is exactly 0 at b = 64.",
    "The orange arrow is the next mini-batch step, the way the ball will move. The grey arrow is −learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn at their true length in bowl units (scale 1).",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or when loss goes above 12 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl (diverged). Off-bowl positions and heights are capped on screen. The loss readout stays true.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed and a counter that keeps going across new runs. Reset replays the same sequence. While playing, the ball takes 5 updates per second at speed 1.",
    "The trail marks where the ball lands after each step, up to 40 points. The kept ghost path also holds up to 40."
  ]
}